UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_subject = 'Thankyou!', post_text = 'ok. Thanks for the feadback! Zell: Thanks dude, i appreciate it... Mad Maniak: SteamaMP uses the Steam built in MP3 console command. Really, it has no interaction wiht the game itself or anyting in memory. It just writes a .cfg file. [] I suppose Steam*AMP* was a bit misleading... I needed sometihng that immediately associated it with playing music. Circuitous: Make sure you give it the correct directory. Thats would be echoed to you becuase it canno find the file (\'mp3command.cfg\'). Likely scenario is it is outputting to the wrong directory. On first run, the path defaults to the steam Half-Life dir. You\'ll no doubt hve to change this. Thanks again, I hope you find SteamAMP useful.' WHERE post_id = 166714 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'I was in the right directory. At any rate, it works for CS now, and so I guess that\'s good enough.' WHERE post_id = 166745 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Very nice - I like it. You got a memory leak somewhere when you close the proggy with the X, so make sure you unload everything properly. Other than that, works fine for me. I\'d keep working on this as it has the potential to turn into a real nice utility when it\'s all squeaky clean. Also, with the F6 mp3 stop command, there is no need really to have that generate the config file. Obviously for playing an mp3 it is needed, but for stopping it you could just add that directly to the userconfig.cfg and get rid of it having to write to the mp3commands.cfg file. Those are my suggestions, which isn\'t much at all really. Good work mate. EDIT: Oh, and add in support for turning up and down the \'MP3Volume\' command and the \'VOLUME\' command, including buttons to auto mute either one. Like F5 to mute in game sound, and F4 to mute MP3 sound etc. That\'d be pretty dudey! For example, you could use: ---------------------------- alias +mp3VolumeChanger "bind F1 MP3Volume 0.1; bind F2 MP3Volume 0.2; bind F3 MP3Volume 0.3; bind F4 MP3Volume 0.4; bind F5 MP3Volume 0.5; bind F6 MP3Volume 0.6; bind F7 MP3Volume 0.7; bind F8 MP3Volume 0.8; bind F9 MP3Volume 0.9; bind F10 MP3Volume 1; bind F11 MP3Volume 0" alias -mp3VolumeChanger "<rebind all keys to normal settings etc>" alias +VolumeChanger "bind F1 Volume 0.1; bind F2 Volume 0.2; bind F3 Volume 0.3; bind F4 Volume 0.4; bind F5 Volume 0.5; bind F6 Volume 0.6; bind F7 Volume 0.7; bind F8 Volume 0.8; bind F9 Volume 0.9; bind F10 Volume 1; bind F12 Volume 0" alias -VolumeChanger "<rebind all keys to normal settings etc>" bind F12 +mp3VolumeChanger bind F11 +VolumeChanger ------------------------------------------------- With that, you\'d hold down F12 and then press F1 to set MP3Volume to 0.1, or F2 to set it to 0.2 ect up until F10 with sets it to 1.0. If you press F11 it mutes it by setting to 0. Letting go of the key rebinds F1 to F10 with their normal commands. F11 does the same with Volume settings, and F12 this time will mute it. I havn\'t tested this script but you get the drift. I\'ll shutup now because I\'m rambling on.' WHERE post_id = 167269 AND is_historical = 1;
