UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_subject = 'Open Letter to Valve concerning netcode', post_text = 'To whomever it may concern, Having been an avid counter-strike player for approximately 3 years now, I am baffled by the change that has been distributed to all Steam enabled users this past day. It is completely undocumented, as is the new c4timer-icon by the way, and rather confusing. It is a common opinion that "all ms is bad ms", allthough I myself am used to having it due to an ISDN connection, alot of people are rather frustrated having gone from what they call "0ms" to approximately 50. Having not experienced any noticeable change in the amount myself I proceeded to toy around with my netcode settings, and the results were mildly put shocking and confusing. In all past versions I can remember cl_updaterate has been set as high as possible. This rendered the best possible connection and a feeling of "0 ms" due to the exclusion of the figure in the netgraph once the value dropped beneath a certain value. I noticed now that the effect of configuring cl_updaterate is the exact opposite of the before-mentioned versions . An updaterate of 100 renders a high ms, and an updaterate of 10 renders a showing of so-called "0 ms". My question is therefore if you could provide me with: 1) An explanation of what "ms" actually is, once and for all. 2) An explanation of how cl_updaterate affects the value, and what changes have been made to the setting since the previous version. Any additional information regarding changes in the netcode, and also any good explanations of how it all works that you happen to have lying around would be greatly appreciated. Best regards and thanks for a good game, Daniel Blekkan' WHERE post_id = 243671 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = '*bump* C\'mon guys, help me out here [] But keep it mature please, we want Valve to respond, not ignore us due to excessive whining or use of profanity.' WHERE post_id = 244178 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'I agree with all the infomation the above person has been saying i agree that we all need to know what exatcly cl_cmdrate and cl_updaterate do i use to get 0 ms with 100 100 but now i get 40-50 this feels weird and a lil slower for some reason so i also ask what is it these 2 commands do and next time plz explain yourself better so this community can get the best out of Counter-Strike Gaming.... Dark-Force [k]illerz < Counter-Strike New Zealand Clan >' WHERE post_id = 244222 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = '"cl_updaterate/cl_cmdrate Why the default values are relatively low is because the game is designed to work for players with every type of connection and give them a smooth game play over the Internet. Another reason is that HLDS should work on most computers and not cause an immense load on the CPU. As explained in my previous net code column the cl_updaterate and cl_cmdrate controls the server-client and client-server traffic. The higher values, the more traffic. However using low values won\'t create an inaccurate picture of the gaming world (that will only occur if the client starts to drop a lot of packets) because there doesn\'t need to be a one-to-one correspondence with the way the Half-Life engine works. The bad thing about using too high cl_updaterate and cl_cmdrates is that: Your client bandwidth doesn\'t handle all the traffic and you receive choke (as explained above). The server you are playing on has to work under a lot higher pressure since much smaller and more movements are sent to the server for calculation which consumes CPU power and can cause server-lag. The important factor here is to set values depending on personal choice and what your bandwidth and computer actually can handle. On LAN and in tournaments, these values should be set high for best consistency." Taken from # http://www.schroet.com/features.php...2&insider=0 (http://www.schroet.com/features.php?id=34&page=2&insider=0) # Author: bds If you look at that page, bds explains everything about the netcode. You need to register for Shroet to read the rest, makes interesting reading' WHERE post_id = 244249 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Another point: if your getting loss on a server dont bother playing at all, its sommit that isnt worth playing with as it is pure packet loss, blah blah' WHERE post_id = 244253 AND is_historical = 1;
