UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'quote:Originally posted by Mac182 Yer, but it is an explaination of the 1.6 code apparently http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846 (http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846) as thats where my source came for and it clearly states "BDS EXPLAINS NETCODE IN CS 1.6" Nvd. gl waiting from a explaination here, not many admins ( if any ) have technical knowledge of the HL net code. OMG Either read his posts, or STFU!' WHERE post_id = 244302 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Opacus least i "tired" to post sommit constructive, instead of n00bie flammerage by the likes of you, you have posted twice on this and contributed jack, so it is u that should stf*u tbh. Sad little flammers like you need to get a life tbh, instead of trying to just increase his/her amount of posts. If you pld cs for 3 yrs you should understand things like cmdrate updaterate and the old pushlatency. Valve have stated they have NOT changed the netcode at all for the 1.6 steam transition or any updates since. To answer you 2 questions: 1)"ms" is the time it takes for you to send a packet to the server and recieve a packet in return. a ping of 50 ms means it will take 50 milli seconds to send and recieve a packet, however, overheads such as loss and choke can ditort this and cause packet loss. Increasing cl_cmdrate and cl_updaterate can increase the rate of the packets sent and recived, hence the increase in in/out rates on your netgraph, however, can increase your ping from say 35->50 as the packets are more frequent. It is more preferable to have a higher ping but more packet communication with the server in my experience, the bullet reg is generally better, so not all ms is bad ms. 2)cl_updaterate outcome has not changed since the previous versions, however, value seemed to have reversed the effects on the ms value reported by the netgrapah. Low updatrates now appear to Lower ping vice versa. ppl are reporting sluggishness, however the netcode has not changed only the crappy output on ppls netgraph, so ppl with 101/101 may say their ping is higher clientside, where infact it isnt. All you should do is tinker with these values and get the flatest netgraph possible that is the best way to get the best/fastest connection possible imho, personally i have v/sync on, 60 HZ refresh, 60/60/20000. Last thing if you can find the servers sv_maxupdaterate as there isnt much point whacking it to 101 when its 70, you will probably get choke or sommit for over requesting packets. Let me know if this helps or if anything i said is baffling or wrong blah blah.' WHERE post_id = 244413 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Well, and I am really trying to keep my temper here, could you please explain the difference between the value of ms in our "crappy netgraphs" as you so eloquently put it and the ping in the scoreboard? And for the record, it is in no way a more accurate latency measure. I play on an ISDN connection every day, and by fiddling with cl_cmd and update- rate I can lower my "ms" to 10 and even under, granted suffering from choke but still it is under 10. My ping, shown on the scoreboard, however will at this amount of data have risen to at least 80. Also I know that even with absolutely no dataflow whatsoever a ping of 10ms is absolutely impossible considering my connection and my ISP\'s route to the server. Therefore I would like to point out that ms is in fact an abbreviation of milliseconds, and that it could be measuring anything for all we know. This point is precisely why I have asked Valve for an explanation and answer to my question.' WHERE post_id = 244453 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Well I cant really add any constructive thinking to this post. But I would like to add my story of what happened after the latest update 26/27-11-03. Almost everybody I know, and me have experienced a ms of about 20 konstantly, and only after the new update. And no matter what I do I can\'t get rid of it. rate cl_updaterate, cmdrate and so on doesn\'t change anything. I got a 512/512 ADSL connection and it have never been a problem before. So it would be nice to know what they changed, in the last update apart from showing the VAC client side, player shadows(can they be disabled). weapons and so on. Because I see many other things happening. Like keys no longer working, funny text appearing when trying to use buyscript. So all in all nice to know things :-) Thanks Malum' WHERE post_id = 244459 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Im sorry, but you aren\'t gonna get a member of valves team reply to a post on a public message board policed by steam. Think about it :/ and dont go loosing your cool i am trying to help you clarify things. Scoreboard = actual ping to the server. 80ms in the case you described. Netgraph = is the ping your client expects given the amount of data you send and the frequency of it. It will lie as the calculations made dont take into consideration server location / net traffice etc, it is only calculations made by your pc alone given the data it sends. You rightly said you can lower the value appearing on the netgraph at the expense of getting a higher ping on the scoreboard. You get a higher ping on the scorebaord because you are sending more packets and recieving more (more data transfer), and your home pc will think you will have a lower ping because it is sending lots more packets, however, the increased frequencies of packet sending will give a better bullet reg and smoothness to the game. Lowering the rate values to say 10/10 will give you a nice low ping on the scoreboard, and a horrible "netgrpah" ping however you are sending nothing in the way of packets. You get a lower ping to the server cause its like playing tennis with 1 ball. I have already defined \'ms\' the time it takes to send and recieve a packet from the server in milliseconds. Your \'ms\' is just really the speed your packets come back to you and tinkering the cmd and update can send more of these packets, sending more takes longer for em to come back unless you have the bandwidth' WHERE post_id = 244496 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'quote:Originally posted by Mac182 Lowering the rate values to say 10/10 will give you a nice low ping on the scoreboard, and a horrible "netgrpah" ping however you are sending nothing in the way of packets. This is my point exactly. That is the OLD behaviour of cl_updaterate. However now, it behaves the exact opposite. Also, you are describing scoreboard ping as ACTUAL latency and netgraph ms as EXPECTED latency? That doesn\'t make even a bit of sense to me, what would be the point of the netgraph value of ms in that scenario? It has always been my belief that scoreboard ping is the latency reported by hl and netgraph ms is the frequency of updates received measured in milliseconds. This because of the direct relationship between updaterate and ms. If we set our client to update 100 times a second(updaterate), we will be receiving updates more frequently than when requestion 40 updates a second. With the new flipped behaviour of cl_updaterate I am not so sure any longer, and request an explanation, with some form of reference, preferably from a staff-member from Valve. As for your statements, I appreciate the effort, but like I\'ve said before, I would like an explanation of the CHANGE made, and also a clear definition from someone who definately knows as to what ms really is.' WHERE post_id = 244504 AND is_historical = 1;
