UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_subject = 'Bugs and errors reporting', post_text = 'Greetings! Wandering around on the forum, I noticed there is a specific thread for reporting encountered bugs and errors. Though this may be suitable in a sufficient way, do you not think it would be easier if all reporting of such errors would be centralised and consultable in a single database? In my opinion, this would be better for several reasons, those being: better organisation of bugs (with status for each bug, such as: �Critical\', �Important\', �Normal\', �Minimal\', �Resolved\'); easier consultation of posted bugs; easier and less cluttered research of posted bugs (to avoid duplicate postings, for example); to a certain extent, easier moderation; possible further discussion of each posted bug individually; integration with Steam forum account also possible. This can be quite easily done (with PHP, and MySQL), and if done well could make following of bugs and errors easier. I am imagining the interface could resemble that of Mozilla Bugzilla (http://bugzilla.mozdev.org/) (with this list interface (http://bugzilla.mozilla.org/buglist.cgi?product=Browser,MailNews& bug_status=UNCONFIRMED,NEW,ASSIGNED,REOPENED,RESOL VED&chfield=%5BBug%20creation%5D&chfieldfrom=-0d)) for example (of course, without Bugzilla\'s specific complications). Thank you for your answers on this subject. Thomas.' WHERE post_id = 525015 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'This really should belong in the Suggestions & Ideas forum, but who am I to complain. ^_^ Yeah this sounds like a great idea though. And that list looks just like what Steam needs!' WHERE post_id = 525027 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'quote:Originally posted by Clush This really should belong in the Suggestions & Ideas forum Sorry about that, but the bugs reporting thread is in this forum and I did not notice the Suggestions & Ideas forum. Perhaps the latter is only for actual Steam suggestions, and not forum suggestions � but I\'ll let a moderator see to that. I am pleased you are appreciative of the idea, I think it really could make bugs reporting that much more easy and organised (which is always a plus). I\'m currently working on a script lateral to a vBulletin forum so I could integrate bugs reporting to the forum account (if I were chosen to accomplish the task of writing this, of course).' WHERE post_id = 525041 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Hehe sorry I didn\'t totally mean it, just that everyone else says it, why can\'t I! [] Ahh you\'re working on a script? Sweet! I\'d love to see how it\'s getting along sometime.' WHERE post_id = 525045 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'I have made an psudo-campaigne about getting better bug reporting for Steam. I\'ve layed out a number of ideas in this thread (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=72426) and just the other day I PMed Waldo about what possible options are for better bug reporting (he has not yet responded).' WHERE post_id = 525063 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'I understand your idea, but I am thinking of a separate, specialised interface specifically for bug reporting (please see the link to the list interface in my initial post to have an idea of the kind of interface I am imagining would be adequate for the system). This would allow not only for the bug postings to be more specific (with system specifications, for example) but also for the organisation to be easier for both users and moderators.' WHERE post_id = 525088 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'I concur with the Buzilla-ish interface (and included a mention to it in my PM to Waldo), but I am concerned about the potential abuse of the system. The current Bug Report thread is plagued by flames, inaccurate bugs or inappropriate bugs (things that are not really Steam bugs). I do understand the inherent organizational improvement that such a system would bring and perhaps that is a good first step, but I am looking toward creating a bug reporting community that is competent and dedicated and most of all, effective and efficient. A new interface would defiantly help with this, but I am aiming at a new community; one shielded from the flak of unwashed masses. But perhaps I am overreacting. A bugzilla-like system would, as you mention, make it easier for mods to delete frivolous information. Perhaps a drastic social overhaul (such as the one I\'m suggesting) is not needed.' WHERE post_id = 525133 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'In my opinion, the organisational improvement such a system could also bring would be that the bug listing would be isolated from other posts and in this sense problems within the bug listing could be noticed more easily, and hence dealt with faster and more efficiently. Since a separate table for the status of each forum member could be created and updated progressively. This would allow for several improvements upon the traditional bug reporting method, such as: moderators only for the bug listing and not for the forums (and vice-versa, if the need be an issue); warnings and bans could be issued to trouble-makers on the bug listing, without effect on the users\' forum account. This way, the bug listing could prove to be a much more efficient and practical way to enlist and index bugs � open to all users with a Steam forum account � while maintaining a customisable level of protection against eventual problems that could arise (with flamebait, etc...).' WHERE post_id = 525162 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Vexware, I think you have the solution. So... how do we make it happen? Option 1 Convince Valve to spend the time on the system. This would require some well-phrased e-mails to the right people (Waldo, Erik, and Taylor come first to mind) and a good deal of luck. Option 2 Spend the time to make the system for Valve. I don\'t envision Valve ever allowing this, but it is worth a hypothetical mention (considering that they may be too busy to make the system themselves). Option 3 Spend the time to make our own system, independent of Steampowered.com\'s forums. Again, this is a hypothetical solution if Valve doesn\'t have the time. It would require a group of entrepreneur bug-trackers to register a domain, set up the system and actively maintain a high-quality bug database. The pros are that we could be as discriminating as we want and we can have complete control over the system\'s capabilities. The cons are the lack of integration with the Steam forums, the potential lack of integration with Valve (no progress information) and to possibility that Valve would ignore us altogether, though I highly doubt that (as long as we have good bug-reporting). It is worth mentioning that, if we go this route, we might find some help from Valve in the form of server space (if we asked very, very nicely). Option 4 Publish an "official" bug list. If we cannot convince Valve to make such a system and we cannot make one ourselves, we could create a private bug-tracking group and publish our list of bugs however often is required. If we register our own site and make a precedent of reliably reporting significant bugs in a professional manner, it is very likely that Valve will give a serious look at any bugs we report. The pros are that all of the relevant bugs are coalesced into one, easy-to-read document and there is a good chance we could quickly gather the respect of the community and Valve. The major con is the closed nature of the group. The group members are not likely to find every bug to be found and this would necessitate scouring the Steam forums for other bugs. We could also set up an e-mail address for the general community to mail in their bugs for inclusion in the list. If option 1 is not possible, I see this as being the next best idea in terms of being realistically doable. These are the solutions I have. Please respond with reactions, alternatives, or amendments.' WHERE post_id = 525523 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Lunchtimemama, All perfectly workable options, but I think that maybe you should develop your own system first to test it out and improve on it before pouncing it on Valve. Just a thought, so please entertain me on this, but what about a bug submission form at the top (like by Status or whatever) and then developing a mod to install into the board which pulls down the moderated submissions into a forum, so that people can check it to see if the bug has been fixed, or being fixed etc. If you guys need some help, I\'ll try and lend a helping hand where ever possible! PM meh for contact details. []' WHERE post_id = 525581 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'In my opinion, there are already there two options of which we may omit consideration: the first and fourth options. I think we can forget about the first option for several reasons, the most important being that it would be both time-consuming to send several e-mails and waiting for the (probably negative) answers and that these important people will most probably decline our proposition to have them write the system due to their important workload. Also, I believe we may forget the fourth option, since I have the skills (in PHP and MySQL) necessary to write such a system. With the omission of the first and fourth options, let\'s consider the intermediate propositions. Option 2 This is the path I was rather thinking of, because as I have said, I do have the skills to write such a script and have it integrated to the already existing Steam web platform. I am hoping that we can get some attention from the Steam web team, and subsequently enter a frame of negociation with the team about the integration of the bug listing project to the Steam website. Option 3 This is a path we could consider should the previous option not be a success. The obvious disadvantages of this solution are of course the inexistence of integration to the already existing Steam web platform, and the eventual lack of attention from the Steam web team itself. We could of course create a community-driven website, but I think this is running a risk as perhaps the Steam team would ignore the site and instead continue concentrating on the input in the Steam forum. Ideally, the best option would be the second one you listed, because this would mean full support from the Steam web team and officialisation of the resource, so it would be considered a serious bugs database. Whichever the option, I hone the skills needed to create such a system to that is not where we need to focus; we should rather put the limelight on attracting the Steam web team\'s attention by showing our motivation and our capability in producing such a system and demonstrating how it improves upon the bugs reporting scheme already in place. For this, we first need to start by writing a basic version of the script and presenting them the test version so they can make up an opinion of their own.' WHERE post_id = 525596 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Perhaps we could first execute Option 4 (while Option 3 is being developed), then move to Option 3, then, upon it\'s success, integrate our system with Valves and finally arrive at Option 2. What do you think? NOTE: Clush, check your PMs. Also, anyone else interested in bug-reporting-reform, feel free to PM me with contact info as well.' WHERE post_id = 525597 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Vexware, both you and Lunchtimemama have some solid ideas, and I can see there\'s a good chance creating a system would work. Vexware, are you a part-time/full-time/hobby programmer? Not questioning your skills or anything! BTW check your PM in a minute or so.' WHERE post_id = 525604 AND is_historical = 1;
