UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Gabe Newell himself said Direct3D, in its 9th version, is more mature than OpenGL. Because of its ready-made nature, it\'s also quicker to develop games on it. OpenGL is, inevitably, on its way out; the Doom III engine will keep it alive for a few more years, but Unreal Engine 3.0 is likely to supplant it completely, destroying OpenGL and being crowned the king of DirectX9.0. And to answer the original question, there will not be an OpenGL renderer for Half-Life 2 because there is no urgent need for it. Other than Linux abilities, what benefit is there to switch to OpenGL, considering it could take years to completely recode the engine to OpenGL.' WHERE post_id = 1656618 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'HL2 is D3D only and if you could enable OpenGL then it would look very bad commpared to what it is now since OpenGL is an outdated rendering method. D3D supports much nicer particle fx and other things such as reflection and bumbmapping much more easyly so unless OpenGL 2.0 can get its act togather when it will be released, D3D will rule the gaming world got to love micro$oft' WHERE post_id = 1656648 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'FYI for anyone who doesn\'t remember... The original alpha leak from E3 also had an OpenGL renderer built right in. It supported rudimentary shaders (but this was EXTREMELY early in HL2\'s overall development). It wasn\'t the same one from HL1 just left over. Valve later worked much more closely with Microsoft, touted DX9, and also made a very lucrative marketing arrangement with ATI to promote that. But technically there\'s no reason OpenGL can\'t do anything HL2 does right now. The API is different (many would say much simpler and robust), but the 2.0 spec was recently ratified and released, and shaders have been in OpenGL games for a while now. Before Doom 3, there was Tenebrae. We had simple shaders in Call of Duty, and several other games, and things like bumpmapping have been around since 1999/2000! I remember one such game was Slave Zero and it\'s "EVBM" or environmental bumpmapping. I\'m not sure if that had a dual DX7/OpenGL render or not back then. Now UT2003/2004 may not be quite as impressive as Doom3/HL2, but it\'s material system works under both a full DX8/9 renderer and an OpenGL driver (for compatibility and Linux support). The OpenGL 2.0 spec allows for a lot of flexibility, so I wouldn\'t be surprised to see licensed D3 engine games in the future support a newer full OGL2 renderer (D3 uses a custom written driver by Carmack consisting of many OGL2 features under an ARB2 path).' WHERE post_id = 1656875 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'HL2 would not look as beautiful in OpenGL.' WHERE post_id = 1656894 AND is_historical = 1;
UPDATE phpbb_posts SET post_text = 'Surprising how simple people can get brainwashed by companies like valve and M$. D3D is in no way better then OGL. It\'s just a different API, and every game that supports both has been faster in OGL so far, including Far Cry (i got a GF4 Ti4200, it might be different for your card). I got about 30-40 fps in D3D and +/- 100 in OGL, and i think OGL even looked better, but that\'s just an opinion, and you may not agree to that of course. Also, dont forget that hl1/cs runs pretty crap on D3D, and the OGL renderer is clearly superior. All that arguments about cheating are also complete rubbish, cause you can write hacks no matter what graphics API you use. Also, Unreal 3.0 *will* have an opengl renderer. Don\'t forget that D3D is limited to Windows OS\'s. Linux and Mac users will need an opengl renderer anyway. I think ID, with their Doom 3, made a wise choice to go with opengl (an open standard) instead of the proprietary D3D. The reason Valve chose to go with D3D-only, is because of their ties with MS, which is always a bad thing. MS doesnt care about anything but themselves and their own money, and valve probably removed the opengl renderer to do MS a favor. Valve wouldve been better off without that Gabe dude...' WHERE post_id = 1657109 AND is_historical = 1;
